WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.000
<v Susanne>The perfect steak isn't luck.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:07.575
<v Peter>It's a number.

3
00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:08.760
<v Peter>Let's find yours.

4
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:13.290
<v Peter>Hi, I'm Peter.

5
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.550
<v Susanne>And I'm Susanne.

6
00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:20.880
<v Susanne>Today it's all about beef steaks – and a whole roast beef.

7
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.880
<v Peter>Cooked exactly the way you like them.

8
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.591
<v Susanne>Our line-up: fillet, strip loin, rib-eye, T-bone, rump steak and flank

9
00:00:29.591 --> 00:00:32.689
<v Susanne>– plus a whole strip loin for roast beef.

10
00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:37.980
<v Susanne>The names change from country to country – you'll find them on screen.

11
00:00:39.448 --> 00:00:43.612
<v Peter>But the name matters less than this: the thickness.

12
00:00:43.612 --> 00:00:49.000
<v Peter>A thin steak and a thick one need a completely different approach.

13
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.738
<v Susanne>First, doneness.

14
00:00:50.738 --> 00:00:57.000
<v Susanne>There are five levels – and each one has its own core temperature.

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.644
<v Susanne>Rare: forty-eight degrees Celsius, a hundred and eighteen Fahrenheit

16
00:01:02.644 --> 00:01:04.572
<v Susanne>– deep red and very soft.

17
00:01:04.572 --> 00:01:09.584
<v Susanne>Medium rare: fifty-four, or a hundred and twenty-nine – rosy red,

18
00:01:09.584 --> 00:01:11.281
<v Susanne>warm and really juicy.

19
00:01:11.281 --> 00:01:17.142
<v Susanne>Medium: fifty-eight, or a hundred and thirty-six – pink all the way through.

20
00:01:17.142 --> 00:01:23.002
<v Susanne>Medium well: sixty-three, or a hundred and forty-five – just a hint of pink.

21
00:01:23.002 --> 00:01:29.480
<v Susanne>And well done: seventy and above, that's a hundred and fifty-eight – cooked through.

22
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.067
<v Peter>The only way to know for sure: a thermometer.

23
00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:39.740
<v Peter>In from the side, right into the thickest part – and never against the bone.

24
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:47.500
<v Peter>Whole steaks and roasts can stay pink inside – as long as every side is well seared.

25
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.071
<v Peter>Minced beef: always cook it through.

26
00:01:50.071 --> 00:01:55.786
<v Peter>Cooking for small children, pregnant women or someone with a weak immune system?

27
00:01:55.786 --> 00:01:56.500
<v Peter>Well done.

28
00:01:57.044 --> 00:02:00.974
<v Susanne>Now the golden rule: thickness decides the method.

29
00:02:00.974 --> 00:02:06.083
<v Susanne>Under two and a half centimetres – about an inch – it's pan only.

30
00:02:06.283 --> 00:02:11.333
<v Susanne>Up to four centimetres, we cook forward: sear first, then the oven.

31
00:02:11.333 --> 00:02:16.683
<v Susanne>Anything thicker, and every roast, goes reverse: oven first, sear last.

32
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.864
<v Susanne>Whatever the method: pat the meat dry – a wet steak steams, a dry one sears.

33
00:02:23.864 --> 00:02:29.034
<v Susanne>Salt right before cooking, or a day ahead, uncovered in the fridge.

34
00:02:29.034 --> 00:02:31.040
<v Susanne>Pepper goes on at the end.

35
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.802
<v Peter>Method one: thin steaks.

36
00:02:34.802 --> 00:02:37.430
<v Peter>This rump steak is two centimetres.

37
00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.560
<v Peter>Pan as hot as it gets, a little high-heat oil – and in.

38
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.752
<v Peter>About two minutes per side for medium rare.

39
00:02:45.752 --> 00:02:46.520
<v Peter>Flip – done.

40
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.760
<v Peter>Give it two or three minutes' rest.

41
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.335
<v Peter>Method two: forward.

42
00:02:52.335 --> 00:02:55.140
<v Peter>Our strip loin is three centimetres thick.

43
00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.480
<v Peter>Sear it hard, about two minutes per side – and the fat edge, too.

44
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:06.260
<v Peter>If you like: a knob of butter or margarine, garlic and thyme – and baste.

45
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.795
<v Peter>Then into the oven at a hundred and ten degrees Celsius

46
00:03:10.795 --> 00:03:12.625
<v Peter>– two hundred and thirty Fahrenheit.

47
00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:16.640
<v Peter>For medium rare, I take it out at fifty-one – that's a hundred and twenty-four.

48
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.761
<v Peter>It keeps cooking while it rests.

49
00:03:20.761 --> 00:03:25.000
<v Peter>Five minutes – and we're at fifty-four, a hundred and twenty-nine Fahrenheit.

50
00:03:26.715 --> 00:03:28.750
<v Susanne>Method three: reverse.

51
00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:32.266
<v Susanne>This T-bone is five centimetres thick.

52
00:03:32.266 --> 00:03:39.390
<v Susanne>So: oven first – a hundred and ten Celsius, two hundred and thirty Fahrenheit

53
00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:40.500
<v Susanne>– on a rack.

54
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.354
<v Susanne>Slowly up to forty-nine Celsius – a hundred and twenty Fahrenheit.

55
00:03:45.354 --> 00:03:48.520
<v Susanne>That's five degrees Celsius short of our target.

56
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.968
<v Peter>Then a screaming-hot pan: one minute per side, plus the edges.

57
00:03:53.968 --> 00:03:56.400
<v Peter>The sear takes it the rest of the way.

58
00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.171
<v Susanne>See the difference?

59
00:03:59.171 --> 00:04:02.275
<v Susanne>Forward leaves a grey band under the crust.

60
00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:07.400
<v Susanne>Reverse is pink almost from edge to edge – and needs only a short rest.

61
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.028
<v Susanne>And the biggest of them all: a whole roast beef.

62
00:04:13.028 --> 00:04:17.560
<v Susanne>Score the fat cap, salt it – and it goes reverse, too.

63
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.667
<v Peter>One and a half kilos take about two hours – but trust the thermometer,

64
00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:23.400
<v Peter>not the clock.

65
00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.183
<v Peter>Sear it on every side, ends included.

66
00:04:28.183 --> 00:04:30.220
<v Peter>Then let it rest for ten minutes.

67
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.740
<v Susanne>Slice it thin, across the grain.

68
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.880
<v Susanne>Rosy all the way through.

69
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.342
<v Peter>So: measure the thickness, pick your method, trust your thermometer

70
00:04:43.342 --> 00:04:45.480
<v Peter>– and take it out a little early.

71
00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.170
<v Susanne>Rare or well done – it's just a number.

72
00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:56.670
<v Peter>See you next time – and enjoy your steak!
